 📚 Queue 

 🔸 1. What is a Queue?
A Queue is a linear data structure that follows the FIFO (First In, First Out) principle.
 Enqueue → Add element to the rear
 Dequeue → Remove element from the front
 🧠 RealLife Analogy:
Think of a line at a ticket counter — the person who enters the line first gets served first.

 🔸 2. Basic Operations
| Operation    | Description                            | Time Complexity |
|  |  |  |
| `enqueue(x)` | Add `x` to the rear                    | O(1)            |
| `dequeue()`  | Remove item from the front             | O(1)            |
| `peek()`     | View the front item without removing   | O(1)            |
| `isEmpty()`  | Check if the queue is empty            | O(1)            |
| `isFull()`   | (If fixed size) Check if queue is full | O(1)            |

 🔸 3. Types of Queues

 ✅ a. Linear Queue
 Basic queue using array or linked list.
 Simple to implement, but arraybased queues may waste space after repeated dequeues.

 ✅ b. Circular Queue
 Arraybased queue where `rear` wraps around to the beginning using:
  `rear = (rear + 1) % size`
 Efficient space usage.

 🔍 Full Condition:
 Queue is full: `(rear + 1) % size == front`
 Queue is empty: `front == 1`

 ✅ c. Priority Queue
 Elements are dequeued based on priority, not arrival order.
 Usually implemented using heaps:
   Min Heap: smallest element has highest priority
   Max Heap: largest element has highest priority

 ✅ d. Deque (DoubleEnded Queue)
 Insertion and deletion can be done from both front and rear.
 Useful in sliding window problems, palindrome checks, etc.

 🔸 4. Implementation Approaches

 ✅ ArrayBased Queue

int[] queue = new int[size];
int front = 0;
int rear = 1;
int count = 0;

void enqueue(int x) {
  if (count == size) // full
  queue[++rear % size] = x;
  count++;
}

void dequeue() {
  if (count == 0) // empty
  front = (front + 1) % size;
  count;
}

 ✅ Linked ListBased Queue

 Use a Node class.
 Maintain `head` (front) and `tail` (rear) pointers.
 Dynamically resizable.

 ✅ Java Builtin Queue

Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
queue.add(1);     // enqueue
queue.poll();     // dequeue
queue.peek();     // front element
queue.isEmpty();  // check empty

 🔸 5. Queue Use Cases

| Application             | Description                              |
|  |  |
| BFS in graphs/trees     | Traverse nodes levelbylevel            |
| CPU/Job scheduling      | Tasks executed in the order received     |
| Message queues          | Used in async programming, event systems |
| Sliding window problems | Maximum/minimum in a range using deque   |
| LRU Cache (with deque)  | Least Recently Used caching mechanism    |

 🔸 6. Important Patterns

 🌀 BreadthFirst Search (BFS)
 Use queue to explore neighbors level by level in a graph/tree.

 🔁 Sliding Window
 Use deque to maintain useful elements inside the window range.

 🔀 Stack Using Queues / Queue Using Stacks
 Classic problems to understand queuestack conversion.

